STATUS TONE RULES
<hr>
If Status Tone is used, TRC ENCODE and Wireline Tones cannot be used.


WIRELINE TONE GENERATION RULES
<hr>
If Wild Card tables, containing commands which generate wireline=20
tones, are set up in stations configured for 2-wire wireline=20
connections, then these tables must also take into account that=20
the tones must be muted BEFORE an incoming console call. The muting=20
must be handled in the wild card tables. The WildCard feature will=20
not automatically mute the WildCard generated tones thus an incoming
call CANNOT be detected.



&lt;/content&gt;&lt;/helpitem&gt;&lt;helpitem keyword=3D"motac_hlp"&gt;&lt;=
content&gt;CHANGE/VIEW CODEPLUG MENU HELP

   This section allows the user to view and change the comparator's operati=
ng
   parameters.  Some screens may be disabled based on the current hardware
   configuration.


button Description:

   Help  - Recalls this Help Screen.

   Change and view configuration parameters which are specific to
	 a conventional system configuration.  This screen is disabled if
	 the system type field in the Hardware Configuration screen is not
	 set to CONVENTIONAL.

   Change and view configuration parameters which are specific to a
	 SmartZone system configuration.  This screen is disabled if the
	 system type field in the Hardware Configuration screen is not
	 set to SMARTZONE.

   Change and view configuration parameters which are applied to the
	 voting algorithm for analog signals. This screen is disabled if the
	 system type field in the Hardware Configuration screen is set to
	 CONVENTIONAL and the comparator type field in the Hardware
	 Configuration screen is set to EXPANDED.

   Change and view configuration parameters that are port specific.
	 The link type, link delay, and output levels are among the
	 parameters which are present in this section. This screen is disabled
	 in expanded conventional systems when the chassis number field in
	 the Hardware Configuration Screen is set to SECONDARY1 through
	 SECONDARY4. Port Control parameters may only be modified on a PRIMARY
	 expanded comparator.

                                                                     =20


&lt;/content&gt;&lt;/helpitem&gt;&lt;helpitem keyword=3D"otac_dgen_hlp"&gt;=
&lt;content&gt;CONVENTIONAL SYSTEM PARAMETERS SCREEN HELP

This screen is used to view and modify comparator parameters which are=20
only applicable to conventional system configurations.  Access to this=20
screen is denied if the system type field in the Hardware Configuration=20
screen is not set to CONVENTIONAL.


                                                           =20
  =20

&lt;/content&gt;&lt;/helpitem&gt;&lt;helpitem keyword=3D"otac_voice_rpt_hlp=
"&gt;&lt;content&gt;VOICE REPEAT OPERATION HELP

The voice repeat setting controls the routing of subscriber voice signals
by the comparator.  When voice repeat is set to enabled, received=20
subscriber voice signals will be rebroadcast over the air.  This allows=20
all other subscriber users to receive the signal.

When voice repeat is set to disabled, received subscriber voice signals are
sent only to consoles which are connected to the comparator.  Hence,=20
subscriber users will be unable to speak to one another in systems with the=
=20
voice repeat feature disabled.

In conventional operation, when the Repeater Set Up and Knockdown
wireline commands are sent from a console/DIU to enable or disable voice
repeat operation, these commands will override the current comparator
RSS setting and will change the voice repeat state of the comparator.
Refer to the DIU programming instructions for console commands
(typically 1450 and 1550 Hz function tones) that cause the DIU to send a
repeat enable/disable command to the comparator.

The parameter applies to both analog voice calls and digital voice calls.

The default setting for this field is ENABLED.

&lt;/content&gt;&lt;/helpitem&gt;&lt;helpitem keyword=3D"otac_vote_prior_hl=
p"&gt;&lt;content&gt;VOTE PRIORITY HELP

This parameter is used to control which type of incoming voted signal has
higher priority.  Note that this parameter only affects voted signals.=20
Voted signals are voice/data signals which enter the comparator from a=20
base station.  The user may select one of three configurations.=20

In the explanation below, an analog signal is the equivalent of an analog
voice call.  Digital signals may be either digital voice calls or digital=
=20
data calls in the following explanation.

   FIRST IN - In this configuration, voted analog signals and voted=20
              digital signals have equal priority.  In this case, the=20
              voted signal which arrives at the comparator first will=20
              have the highest priority of any voted signals.  It will=20
              retain the highest voted signal priority until the call ends.=
 =20
              For example, a user makes a digital call on subscriber A. =20
              While subscriber A is keyed, a second user makes an analog=20
              call on subscriber B.  The best (voted) signal from=20
              subscriber A will have priority over the best signal for=20
              subscriber B.  Now suppose subscriber A's call ends and a=20
              new subscriber, C, is keyed up in digital mode.  Subscriber=
=20
              B's voted signal has the highest priority of the voted=20
              signals because out of the two signals, B and C, it was the=
=20
              first to arrive at the comparator. =20

   ANALOG&gt;DIGITAL - In this configuration, voted analog signals will tak=
e=20
                    priority over voted digital signals.  A digital call=20
                    from a subscriber will be preempted by an analog call=
=20
                    from a subscriber in this configuration.

   DIGITAL&gt;ANALOG -  In this configuration, voted digital signals will t=
ake
                     priority over voted analog signals.  A call from an=20
                     analog subscriber will be preempted by a call from a=
=20
                     digital subscriber in this configuration.

The default configuration is FIRST IN.

&lt;/content&gt;&lt;/helpitem&gt;&lt;helpitem keyword=3D"otac_src_base_hlp"=
&gt;&lt;content&gt;SOURCE-BASED PRIORITY HELP

This parameter determines signal priority based on the device that=20
sourced the signal arriving at a wireline port.  This parameter=20
applies to both voted and non-voted signals.  The user may select=20
one of two configurations.

   CONSOLE - Signals arriving from a console will have higher priority=20
             than the signals received from a station.  In this=20
             configuration, a call from a console will preempt a call=20
             from a subscriber.

   REPEAT  - Signals arriving from a station will have higher priority=20
             than the signals received from a console.  In this=20
             configuration, a call from a subscriber will preempt a=20
             call from a console.

Please note that this field applies to voice and data sessions.  Control=20
information from a console such as the "channel change" command will=20
always take priority over signals sourced from a station.
=20
The default configuration is CONSOLE.

&lt;/content&gt;&lt;/helpitem&gt;&lt;helpitem keyword=3D"otac_diu_rpt_hlp"&=
gt;&lt;content&gt;PARALLEL AUDIO OPERATION HELP

This parameter enables or disables the routing of console/DIU port sourced
analog and digital voice and control traffic arriving at the ATAC3000=20
to all other console/DIU ports.  When this parameter is disabled the ATAC30=
00
will not route console/DIU port sourced traffic to other console/DIU ports
but only send this traffic to the station/receiver ports.  When this parame=
ter
is enabled the ATAC3000 will repeat analog and digital voice and control
traffic received from a console/DIU port to all other console/DIU ports
(except the sourcing port) and to station/receiver ports.

WARNING:
If not used properly, this feature can have adverse effects on system opera=
tion.
Users who are not aware of these operational constraints should contact
Motorola=E2=80=99s System Support Center at 1-800-221-7144, or access the=
=20
"ASTRO-TAC 3000 Conventional Parallel Audio Option User Guidelines" (687100=
9P07)
available at Motorola Online (MOL) for detailed user information before ena=
bling
the Parallel Audio feature.

&lt;/content&gt;&lt;/helpitem&gt;&lt;helpitem keyword=3D"console_echo_cance=
l_hlp"&gt;&lt;content&gt;CONSOLE ECHO CANCEL OPERATION HELP

The Console Echo Cancellation Feature provides the capability, for ASTRO
Conventional Systems, to mute inbound audio when the console is transmittin=
g
outbound. This feature is normally used in simplex systems where the transm=
it
frequency is the same as the receive frequency and the transmitting station
and the outlying receivers will receive whatever is being transmitted and r=
eturn
it to the comparator for processing as inbound audio.

The RSS allows this feature to be enabled at any time since the comparator =
has
no knowledge of the RF configuration of the system and relies on the instal=
ler
to judiciously configure the comparator for proper operation. Simplex syste=
ms are
normally configured with the repeat function in the comparator disabled.
This feature can also be enabled when the comparator is configured to repea=
t
and the system is not a Simplex system and the comparator will mute any inb=
ound voice.

This feature only mutes voice; it does not mute inbound data or ASTRO contr=
ol
signaling, such as Call Alert signaling.

&lt;/content&gt;&lt;/helpitem&gt;&lt;helpitem keyword=3D"console_echo_cance=
l_delay_hlp"&gt;&lt;content&gt;CONSOLE ECHO CANCEL DELAY HELP

When Console Echo Cancel feature is enabled, this parameter specifies the
amount of echo cancellation time after the console stops transmitting.

The range is 0 to 2000 miliseconds.  Resolution is in 10 miliseconds.=20

The default is 0 miliseconds.

&lt;/content&gt;&lt;/helpitem&gt;&lt;helpitem keyword=3D"otac_an_rep_hng_hl=
p"&gt;&lt;content&gt;ANALOG VOICE REPEAT HANGTIME HELP

This parameter specifies the amount of time that base stations connected=20
to a comparator will remain keyed-up and transmitting silence at the=20
conclusion of an analog voice call sourced by a subscriber. The period of=
=20
silence may preempted by any incoming signal. =20

This field is ignored if the Voice Repeat Operation field is set to=20
DISABLED.

The range is 0.0 to 15.0 seconds.  Resolution is in tenths of seconds.=20

The default is 2.0 seconds.

&lt;/content&gt;&lt;/helpitem&gt;&lt;helpitem keyword=3D"otac_an_con_hng_hl=
p"&gt;&lt;content&gt;ANALOG VOICE CONSOLE HANGTIME HELP

This parameter specifies the amount of time that base stations connected=20
to the comparator will remain keyed-up and transmitting silence at the=20
conclusion of an analog voice call sourced by a console (DIU port).  The=20
period of silence may preempted by any incoming signal. =20

The range is 0.0 to 15.0 seconds.  Resolution is in tenths of seconds.=20

The default is 0.0 seconds.

&lt;/content&gt;&lt;/helpitem&gt;&lt;helpitem keyword=3D"otac_dig_rep_hng_h=
lp"&gt;&lt;content&gt;DIGITAL VOICE REPEAT HANGTIME HELP

This parameter specifies the amount of time a base station connected to a
comparator will remain keyed-up and transmitting silence at the conclusion
of a digital voice call sourced by a subscriber unit. The period of silence
may preempted by any incoming signal. =20

This parameter is ignored by the comparator if the Voice Repeat Operation
field is set to DISABLED.

The range is 0.0 to 15.0 seconds.  Resolution is in tenths of seconds.=20

The default is 5.0 seconds.

&lt;/content&gt;&lt;/helpitem&gt;&lt;helpitem keyword=3D"otac_dig_con_hng_h=
lp"&gt;&lt;content&gt;DIGITAL VOICE CONSOLE HANGTIME HELP

This parameter specifies the amount of time a base station connected to the
comparator will remain keyed-up and transmitting silence at the conclusion
of a digital voice call sourced by a console.  The period of silence may
be preempted by any incoming signal. =20

The range is 0.0 to 15.0 seconds.  Resolution is in tenths of seconds.=20

The default is 0.0 seconds.

&lt;/content&gt;&lt;/helpitem&gt;&lt;helpitem keyword=3D"otac_dig_datcon_hn=
g_hlp"&gt;&lt;content&gt;DIGITAL DATA CONSOLE HANGTIME HELP

This parameter specifies the amount of time a base station connected to the
comparator will remain keyed-up and transmitting silence at the conclusion
of a digital data call sourced by a console (DIU port).  The period of=20
silence may be preempted by any incoming signal. =20

The range is 0.0 to 15.0 seconds.  Resolution is in tenths of seconds.

The default is 0.0 seconds.

NOTE: This field should not be set to 0 if this is a simulcast system.
&lt;/content&gt;&lt;/helpitem&gt;&lt;helpitem keyword=3D"otac_ht_hdlc_help"=
&gt;&lt;content&gt;HANGTIME HDLC ADDRESS HELP

This parameter specifies which station(s) will transmit hangtime
generated by the Comparator.  The field can be configured such =20
that hangtime is transmitted by all connected stations or=20
steered to a single connected station. =20

For system configurations in which simulcast is enabled and
data capability is used, this field should be set to steer hangtime
to the transmitting station associated with the largest number of=20
data users.  The configuration steps for this situation are
as follows:

   1.  Determine which transmitting station connected to the=20
       Comparator communicates with the largest number of data
       users during normal system use.  =20

   2.  Determine the HDLC address for the station selected in=20
       step 1.  The HDLC address can be obtained from the=20
       status panel screen in the Comparator RSS.  The station
       HDLC address can also be determined by executing the=20
       station RSS, reading in the station's codeplug, and=20
       examining the site number field in the hardware config
       screen.

   3.  Enter the station's HDLC in the Hangtime HDLC Address field.
             =20
   4.  Save the codeplug to the Comparator.

For non-simulcast systems or voice-only systems, this field should be
set to 3F.   =20

The field value of 3F indicates that all transmit capable stations
will transmit hangtime generated by the comparator.

The range for this parameter is 0 to 3F (hex).

The default value for this parameter is 3F (hex).

&lt;/content&gt;&lt;/helpitem&gt;&lt;helpitem keyword=3D"otac_def_tx_help"&=
gt;&lt;content&gt;DEFAULT TRANSMITTER STEERING ADDRESS HELP

This parameter specifies the address that the comparator
uses when there is no unique address specified in the source
message which the comparator is sending to base stations.
This field will not affect the address used when the signal
destination is a DIU or AEB.

Generally, this field is set to 3F (hex) unless signals are
to be steered to an individual transmitter by default.  The
comparator will use the default transmitter steering address
in the codeplug unless the console operator selects a new
transmit address.  The comparator will revert to the default
transmitter steering address upon power up.

A value of 0 for this field will result in all connected
stations ignoring the signal sent by the comparator.

Values ranging from 2 to 3E (hex) are used to select an
individual transmitter to be used as the default case.  The
address for an individual station can be configured in the
Site Number field on the RSS Hardware Configuration Screen.
The address may also be obtained by reading the codeplug from
the desired stations and recording the address displayed on
the hardware configuration screen.

A value of 3F (hex) for this field corresponds to a broadcast
address.  In this case, all stations connected to the
comparator will transmit the signal sent from the comparator.

The range for this parameter is 0 to 3F (hex).

The default value for this parameter is 3F (hex).

The Comparator's own address is 01, so if data site steering
will be used in the system, all stations must be configured
for a Site Number that is not equal to 01.

&lt;/content&gt;&lt;/helpitem&gt;&lt;helpitem keyword=3D"otac_dtrkotac_hlp"=
&gt;&lt;content&gt;SMARTZONE SYSTEM PARAMETERS SCREEN HELP

This screen is used to view and modify comparator parameters which are
applicable to SmartZone system configurations.  Access to this screen
is denied if the system type field in the Hardware Configuration screen=20
is not set to SMARTZONE.

                 =20

&lt;/content&gt;&lt;/helpitem&gt;&lt;helpitem keyword=3D"otac_failsoft_hlp"=
&gt;&lt;content&gt;FAILSOFT HELP

This field enables/disables the failsoft mechanism.  If the Comparator
cannot detect any OSW's on the CSC link for more than 300 ms, it forces
the radios to switch to pre-assigned channels for Failsoft operation. The
failsoft feature is a fall-back mechanism which allows the channel to=20
operate in a stand-alone state should the system be unable to support
site trunking operation.

Setting this field to DISABLE will cause the Comparator to ignore gaps
in OSW's, and in effect disable the Failsoft function.  With failsoft=20
disabled, a call will not be supported by the comparator when a CSC link
activity failure (lack OSW's for 300 ms) is detected.

With the failsoft field set to ENABLE, calls can be made between radios
programmed to the same failsoft channel when a CSC link activity failure=20
is detected by the comparator.  In addition, the comparator will order=20
the radios assigned to its channel to emit a short tone every ten seconds=
=20
when the channel is in the failsoft state.  During failsoft, a radio user=
=20
will have limited access to the system and will be notified that the system=
=20
is in failsoft by a short tone emitted by the radio every ten seconds.=20

The failsoft feature is only valid for comparators configured for a=20
trunked (SmartZone) system.  The field will be ignored for comparators=20
configured for conventional systems.  The comparator's configuration=20
is determined by the system type field in the hardware configuration=20
screen which is selected from the service menu.

The default setting for this field is DISABLED.

&lt;/content&gt;&lt;/helpitem&gt;&lt;helpitem keyword=3D"otac_fs_mode_hlp"&=
gt;&lt;content&gt;FAILSOFT MODE HELP

This parameter indicates which mode will be used when Failsoft is enabled
(see Failsoft Enable parameter). There are two valid settings: ANALOG and
DIGITAL. The default is DIGITAL.=20

When the Comparator enters failsoft, and the Failsoft Mode parameter
indicates ANALOG, the Comparator will start a silent analog call for the
duration of the Failsoft.  Radio users can still access the system and=20
make a call to radios which revert to this channel during failsoft.  To=20
the radio user, this silent failsoft call should appear and be treated=20
as infinite hangtime.

If the Failsoft Mode parameter indicates DIGITAL, the Comparator will=20
periodically transmit signaling information to the radio which indicates=20
the system is in failsoft.  The signaling information will command the=20
radio to emit a short tone every 10 seconds.  The user may still make=20
a call, but this call is limited to radios which revert to this=20
comparator's channel during failsoft.=20

This field setting should coincide with the signal type field of the=20
failsoft talk group selected in the trunking personality section of=20
the ASTRO Portable or ASTRO Mobile RSS codeplug for a given radio for=20
the failsoft feature to operate correctly.

&lt;/content&gt;&lt;/helpitem&gt;&lt;helpitem keyword=3D"otac_chan_no_hlp"&=
gt;&lt;content&gt;CHANNEL NUMBER HELP

This field must be set to the trunked channel identification number. =20
This number ranges from 1 to 28.

The default is NONE which is an invalid channel identification number.
This is done to prevent multiple comparators from defaulting to the=20
same valid ID, and responding to commands from the CSC that are not=20
intended for them.

The channel number field should agree with the labeled channel number=20
on the front of the comparator (if present). The comparator is connected=20
to the Central Site Controller (CSC) via a serial link.  The CSC serial=20
connector on the comparator is J20 on the backplane.  The serial link=20
leaving the comparator is connected to a data broadcast box.  The data=20
broadcast box is connected to the CSC-TSC link of the CSC.  The channel=20
number field should agree with the channel port on the data broadcast=20
box to which the comparator is connected.=20
=20

Note: This field was handled differently in the ASTRO-TAC. The valid=20
      range for the ASTRO-TAC was 0 - 27, and 28 for invalid/default=20
      Channel Number. ASTRO-TAC's channel 0 corresponds to ASTRO-TAC
      3000's channel 1, ASTRO-TAC's channel 1 corresponds to ASTRO-TAC
      3000's channel 2, etc.

&lt;/content&gt;&lt;/helpitem&gt;&lt;helpitem keyword=3D"otac_dvoice_hlp"&g=
t;&lt;content&gt;ANALOG VOTING PARAMETERS SCREEN HELP
<hr>

This screen allows reading and modification of parameters
which determine the characteristics of the analog voting algorithm.
The algorithm is used when a call is received by the comparator from
a station/receiver or group of stations/receivers. Access to this
screen is denied if system type field in the Hardware Configuration
screen is set to CONVENTIONAL and the comparator type field in the
Hardware Configuration screen is set to EXPANDED.
                    =20

&lt;/content&gt;&lt;/helpitem&gt;&lt;helpitem keyword=3D"otac_vote_samp_hlp=
"&gt;&lt;content&gt;ANALOG VOTING SAMPLE PERIOD HELP

This parameter is used to allow for greater accuracy in=20
determining the noise content of a signal by increasing the=20
interval of time over which the analog signal quality is averaged. =20
It may be used to solve more difficult voting anomalies in particular=20
systems.  Higher values allow for better accuracy in computing the=20
noise content of a signal, but may significantly delay the voting=20
decision between ports.  This parameter is rarely altered.

This parameter is specified in units of milliseconds.

The default value for this parameter is 50 milliseconds.

&lt;/content&gt;&lt;/helpitem&gt;&lt;helpitem keyword=3D"otac_fvote_set_hlp=
"&gt;&lt;content&gt;ANALOG FIRST VOTE SETTLE TIME HELP

This parameter is used to prevent infrastructure induced transients=20
at the beginning of a call from degrading the initial voting decisions=20
for each PTT, and allows the analog voter to obtain a significant audio=20
sample before making a first vote decision.  This is more important in
non-voiced analog signaling systems where the first vote decision is=20
critical. This value should not exceed the period of time between the=20
removal of the modem signal input to the comparator and the beginning=20
of actual analog information (voice or signaling).

This parameter is specified in units of milliseconds.

The default value for this parameter is 40 milliseconds.

&lt;/content&gt;&lt;/helpitem&gt;&lt;helpitem keyword=3D"ot_voicehold_op_he=
lp"&gt;&lt;content&gt;ANALOG VOTE AND HOLD OPERATION HELP

This parameter specifies the state of the analog vote and hold=20
feature.  This feature applies only to analog voice calls.

If the feature is enabled, a single vote will be performed at the=20
beginning of an analog call and result of this vote will be routed=20
for the remainder of the analog call.  The voting algorithm will=20
effectively use an infinite value for the analog first vote hold=20
time for this case.  Hence, the analog first vote hold time field=20
is ignored when the analog vote and hold operation is enabled.  =20

If analog vote and hold operation is disabled,  the signal will be=20
periodically voted throughout the analog call.=20

When this parameter is switched from ENABLED to DISABLED, the Analog=20
First Vote Hold Time parameter is set to its default value.=20

This parameter may be set to ENABLED or DISABLED.

The default value for this parameter is DISABLED.

&lt;/content&gt;&lt;/helpitem&gt;&lt;helpitem keyword=3D"otac_fvote_hold_hl=
p"&gt;&lt;content&gt;ANALOG FIRST VOTE HOLD TIME HELP

This parameter is used to ensure the voted port does not switch for a=20
fixed amount of time after it is chosen as voted port for the
first time.  This parameter is usually set to a value larger than
(length of the MDC preamble + MDC Pretime) - System Truncation.=20
This will prevent the preamble from being corrupted due to the=20
analog voter switching to a new audio source.  This field applies only=20
to the initial vote decision of an analog call unlike the analog vote=20
hold time field which applies to all voting decisions following the=20
initial vote.=20

This field is cannot be edited if the analog vote and hold operation=20
is enabled.  Analog vote and hold operation is equivalent to setting=20
this field to an infinite value so that the signal will be voted only=20
once during the entire analog call.

This parameter is specified in units of milliseconds.

The default value for this parameter is 250 milliseconds.

&lt;/content&gt;&lt;/helpitem&gt;&lt;helpitem keyword=3D"otac_vote_hold_hlp=
"&gt;&lt;content&gt;ANALOG VOTING HOLD TIME HELP

This parameter is used to ensure the voted port does not switch for a=20
fixed amount of time after it is chosen as the voted port.  If the=20
parameter is set too low, the signal may incur frequent interruption=20
or "popping" noises during the call due to the analog voter switching=20
input signals too often.  Conversely, if this parameter is set too high,=20
a less than optimum signal may be routed as output frequently because=20
the hold time will prevent the comparator from switching to the best=20
signal for a long period of time.  This parameter differs from the=20
analog first vote hold time in that the analog first vote hold time=20
applies only to the initial voting decision of an analog call whereas=20
the analog voting hold time applies to all vote decisions after the=20
initial vote.

This parameter is rarely adjusted in the field.

This parameter is specified in units of milliseconds.

The default value for this parameter is 250 milliseconds.

&lt;/content&gt;&lt;/helpitem&gt;&lt;helpitem keyword=3D"otac_vote_swtch_hl=
p"&gt;&lt;content&gt;ANALOG SIGNAL QUALITY DEBOUNCE TIME HELP

This field dictates the amount of time a signal must be of higher=20
quality than the current voted port before it is eligible to be=20
routed as the voted port.  Debouncing the signal quality prevents=20
spikes of strong signaling on a port from inducing a change in=20
the voted port.  This condition typically occurs during transitions=20
on a modem link.
 =20
This parameter is used to ensure that a port with infrastructure-
induced silence is not chosen over a port which continues to carry=20
valid analog audio.  This infrastructure-induced silence is usually=20
present when a subscriber signal fades from a receiver but is still=20
being heard by another receiver in the system.  The time between the=20
squelch in the faded receiver and the transition of the infrastructure=20
link from analog to digital is the amount of silence this parameter=20
will typically be chosen to compensate for.  Higher values will=20
prevent ports with greater amounts of infrastucture-induced silence=20
from being chosen as the voted port, but at the same time will delay=20
the changing of voted ports when the actual signal quality is better. =20
Therefore, care should be taken in choosing a value no higher than=20
is necessary to prevent the voted port from changing on the=20
infrastructure-induced silence.

This parameter is specified in units of milliseconds.

The default value for this parameter is 150 milliseconds.

&lt;/content&gt;&lt;/helpitem&gt;&lt;helpitem keyword=3D"otac_line_trans_hl=
p"&gt;&lt;content&gt;ANALOG LINE TRANSIENT SETTLE HELP

This parameter is used to ensure that any transient on a port during=20
the initial portion of an analog call is excluded from the signal=20
quality measurement.  The line transient settle time should be set to=20
the time period from when carrier detect is lost (falsed) to when the=20
transient settles on a modem link.  Signal quality measurements may=20
be taken after the line transient settle time has expired.  This is=20
the highest priority timer in the analog voting process.  It specifies=20
the amount of time to wait after transitioning to analog before=20
beginning the voting process.=20

This parameter applies to modem links only.  =20

This parameter is specified in units of milliseconds.

The default value for this parameter is 20 milliseconds.

&lt;/content&gt;&lt;/helpitem&gt;&lt;helpitem keyword=3D"otac_vote_hyst_hlp=
"&gt;&lt;content&gt;ANALOG VOTING HYSTERISIS HELP

This parameter is used to ensure that ports with signals of=20
imperceptible difference in noise content are not alternately=20
being chosen as the voted port.  Higher values will require=20
that the noise content of a port with a receive analog signal=20
that is not currently the voted port be significantly less=20
than that of the voted port before that port becomes the voted=20
port.=20

This value should be set so the voted port only switches on=20
signals which have a perceptible difference in noise content. =20
If the difference in noise content between signals is not=20
perceptible, then there is no need to change voted ports.

The analog voting hysterisis field works in conjunction with=20
the analog voting hold time for determining when the analog=20
voter may switch the audio source.  The previous voted port=20
must have been output for the prescribed hold time and the=20
competing audio source must have a noise content which passes=20
the voting hysterisis threshold before a change in the voted=20
port can occur.=20

This parameter is specified in units of decibels with a=20
granularity of 0.1 dB.

The default value for this parameter is 1.1 dB.

&lt;/content&gt;&lt;/helpitem&gt;&lt;helpitem keyword=3D"otac_dportctl_hlp"=
&gt;&lt;content&gt;PORT CONTROL PARAMETERS SCREEN HELP
<hr>

This screen allows the user to read and modify parameters
which define the operation of individual infrastructure ports.
The screen displays the entire parameter list for a single port.
The port is identified by its assigned port number which is presented
as a field on the screen.

The user may scroll through the ports by using the buttons.
If the screen is displaying the configuration for port number X and
the user presses Previous Port, the screen will then display the configurat=
ion
for port number (X - 1).  If the screen is displaying the configuration
for port number X and the user presses Next Port, the screen will display
the configuration for (X + 1).

The user may also modify the Port Number field on the screen to view
the configuration for a desired port.

When the system type field in the Hardware Configuration screen is set to
CONVENTIONAL and the comparator type field in the Hardware Configuration
screen is set to EXPANDED, the user may scroll through all of the comparato=
rs
in the expanded comparator configuration by using the Previous/Next Compara=
tor buttons. The
current comparator port information being viewed is denoted by the Current
Chassis # field at the top of the screen which is not editable. Once a
particular comparator has been selected then the sixteen ports for that
comparator may be modified by using the Previous/Next buttons described abo=
ve. Thus,
if the port information for the PRIMARY chassis is being viewed and the use=
r
presses the Previous Comparator button, then the port information for the S=
ECONDARY4 comparator will be
viewable. Likewise if the port information for the PRIMARY chassis is being
viewed and the user presses the Next Comparator Button, then the port infor=
mation for the SECONDARY1
comparator will be viewable.

IMPORTANT NOTE: In an expanded conventional comparator configuration each
comparator will need to know the port configuration information for all of
the other comparators. Thus, all of the port information for all of the
comparators will need to be configured in one codeplug and this codeplug
will need to be saved to each comparator after making the appropriate
Hardware Configuration screen Chassis Number changes to reflect the
comparator that the codeplug is being saved to.

Button Descriptions:
                                             =20
   Previous Comparator  - Display the port configuration information for th=
e previous
	 comparator. This is only supported for expanded conventional
	 systems.
                       =20
   Previous Port  - Display the port configuration for the previous port.

   Next Port  - Display the port configuration for the next port.          =
        =20
                                             =20
   Next Comparator  - Display the port configuration information for the ne=
xt comparator.
	 This is only supported for expanded conventional systems.
                                                                           =
                              =20


&lt;/content&gt;&lt;/helpitem&gt;&lt;helpitem keyword=3D"otac_port_no_hlp"&=
gt;&lt;content&gt;PORT NUMBER HELP

The port number field is a read-only field which identifies the physical=20
connection in the comparator which is being viewed or modified.  The screen
will display all relevant attributes associated with a specific connection=
